; channel-affordance-band.fk — what each channel in this house can actually answer.
;
; preludes: form-stdlib/core.fk observe/channel-affordance.fk
;
; Fixtures measured 2026-07-22/23: `system_profiler SPAudioDataType` gives the mac builtin
; microphone Input Channels 1; `dumpsys media.audio_flinger` on R5CW20DK17A gives Channel
; count 2, mask 0x3 (front-left, front-right); the mac<->phone clock offset is knowable to
; +/-25 ms from a best-of-15 adb round trip of 50 ms.
;
; Verdict 511 when, on Go / Rust / TypeScript / fkwu:
;     1  the mac mic affords WHEN — one channel is enough for onset
;     2  and affords no bearing at all
;     4  because it has too few channels (why=1), not because of any clock
;     8  the phone's stereo pair affords a bearing
;    16  with 22 samples of lag to work with at 48 kHz
;    32  and 7 samples at 16 kHz — the same geometry, a third of the evidence
;    64  the mac+phone pair affords NO bearing despite having two channels
;   128  and the reason is the clock (why=2), not the channel count
;   256  and nothing here reaches a POSITION — that wants a third channel, same clock
;
; The teaching: affordance is a property of the wire. Bit 64 is the one that costs people
; time — two microphones two metres apart look like the best baseline in the house, and
; they afford nothing, because separate clocks put the arrival difference under the clock's
; own uncertainty. Sound crosses that two-metre baseline in 5.8 ms; the offset is knowable
; to +/-25 ms. The signal is four times smaller than the error bar.
;
; Bits 4 and 128 are separate on purpose: too-few-channels is repaired by adding a channel,
; unshared-clocks by sharing a clock, and a probe that only says "no bearing" leaves the
; caller to guess which — the same reason toggle-admission.fk distinguishes its two refusals.

(do
    (channel-affordance-check))
